namespace Death {
//###==##====#=====--==~--~=~- --- -- -  -  -   -

/** @mainpage

This is an API documentation for Jazz² Resurrection. For user documentation, please visit [project homepage](https://de4th.dev/jazz2/).
Additionaly, the project is hosted on https://github.com/deathkiller/jazz2-native/.

Jazz² Resurrection is reimplementation of the game Jazz Jackrabbit 2 released in 1998. Supports
various versions of the game (Shareware Demo, Holiday Hare '98, The Secret Files and Christmas
Chronicles). Also, it partially supports some features of JJ2+ extension and MLLE. **Online multiplayer**
and **scripting support** (AngelScript) is currently in development, everything else, including
story episodes fully playable in cooperation, is already implemented.

To get in touch with the developers and the community, you can use the official [Discord server](https://discord.gg/Y7SBvkD).

For getting started guide, see @ref building --- and @ref consoles "Building for consoles" for the
console targets. For game-specific API documentation, see @ref Jazz2
namespace. For common utility functions and generic containers, see @ref Death "the shared root namespace".
In addition, @ref CommonBase.h contains common configuration-specific and platform-specific
definitions. For **nCine** game engine documentation, see @ref nCine namespace. The **nCine** game
engine used in this game has been extensively modified, so the original documentation no longer
matches the current implementation.

@section mainpage-platforms Supported platforms

- **Android**
- **FreeBSD**
- **Linux** and other Unix-like systems
- **macOS**
- **Windows**
- **Xbox** (Universal Windows Platform)
- **Web** (WebAssembly) using [Emscripten](https://kripken.github.io/emscripten-site/)
- **Nintendo Switch** using [devkitPro toolchain](https://devkitpro.org/wiki/devkitPro_pacman),
  see @ref consoles-switch
- **Nintendo Wii** and **Nintendo GameCube** using devkitPro toolchain and a fixed-function GX
  rendering backend, see @ref consoles-wii and @ref consoles-gamecube
- **Nintendo 64** using [libdragon](https://github.com/DragonMinded/libdragon) and a fixed-function
  RDP rendering backend, see @ref consoles-n64 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (experimental --- it is by far
  the smallest machine here and does not hold a steady frame rate) @m_endspan
- **Sega Dreamcast** using [KallistiOS](https://github.com/KallistiOS/KallistiOS) and a
  fixed-function PowerVR rendering backend, see @ref consoles-dreamcast
- **PlayStation Portable** using [pspdev](https://github.com/pspdev/pspdev) and a fixed-function
  GU rendering backend, see @ref consoles-psp
- **PlayStation 2** using [ps2dev](https://github.com/ps2dev/ps2dev) and a fixed-function
  Graphics Synthesizer rendering backend, see @ref consoles-ps2
- **PlayStation 3** using [ps3toolchain](https://github.com/ps3dev/ps3toolchain) with **PSL1GHT**
  and a native **libgcm** rendering backend for the RSX, see @ref consoles-ps3
- **PlayStation Vita** using [VitaSDK](https://vitasdk.org/) and a native **sceGxm** rendering
  backend, or OpenGL|ES 2.0 through vitaGL, see @ref consoles-vita
- **libretro** core for RetroArch, see @ref consoles-libretro
- **AmigaOS 3.x** on 68040/68060, PiStorm and Vampire machines, using
  [bebbo's amiga-gcc](https://github.com/bebbo/amiga-gcc) with a bespoke Intuition/RTG window
  backend and the CPU software rasterizer, see @ref amiga-classic
- **AmigaOS 4.1** and **MorphOS** on PowerPC, using their own SDKs through SDL2 and the
  fixed-function `LegacyGL` rendering backend on the OpenGL 1.x each of them provides
  @m_span{m-text m-dim} (MiniGL over Warp3D, resp. TinyGL) @m_endspan, see @ref amiga-os4 and
  @ref amiga-morphos

The code is mainly optimized to run on 64-bit **x86** and **ARM** processors including advanced
instruction sets. Additionally, the application requires a rendering backend --- **OpenGL**
3.3, **OpenGL|ES** 3.0, **WebGL** 2.0, **Direct3D** 11, **Vulkan** and a CPU software rasterizer
are all fully supported and interchangeable, so any of them can be used depending on the target
platform. The platforms above are where that stops being a free choice: six of the consoles have no
programmable shaders at all and are driven by one of five rendering backends written for their
fixed-function graphics hardware @m_span{m-text m-dim} (the Wii and the GameCube share one)
@m_endspan, a sixth such backend drives the fixed-function OpenGL 1.x of the two PowerPC Amigas, while
the PlayStation Vita and the
PlayStation 3 have real shaders and drive their native APIs --- **sceGxm** and **libgcm** ---
directly. The classic Amiga has no usable graphics hardware at all, so there the software rasterizer
is the renderer rather than a fallback. See also @ref consoles-common-features "the list of features each console supports",
which is where the platform differences are the largest.
	
@section mainpage-license License

This project is licensed under the terms of the [GNU General Public License v3.0](https://github.com/deathkiller/jazz2-native/blob/master/LICENSE).
See also @ref third-party for the licenses of the libraries used.

*/

